# Administer the gateway

Tenant administrators configure which upstream providers the Wyrd gateway can
call, with which credentials, and under which policies. Callers never receive
provider authentication material: every read returns a redacted view.

## Resources and routes

| Resource | Routes | Permission |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Provider credential | `PUT/GET/DELETE /v1/admin/gateway/provider-credentials/{name}`, `GET /v1/admin/gateway/provider-credentials`, `POST .../{name}/revoke` | `gateway` read, write (put, revoke), delete |
| Provider deployment | `PUT/GET/DELETE /v1/admin/gateway/provider-deployments/{name}`, `GET /v1/admin/gateway/provider-deployments` | `gateway` read, write, delete |
| Fallback policy | `PUT/GET/DELETE /v1/admin/gateway/fallback-policy` | `gateway` read, write (put, reset) |
| Governance policy | `PUT/GET/DELETE /v1/admin/gateway/governance-policy` | `gateway` read, write (put, reset) |
| Capture policy | `PUT/GET /v1/admin/gateway/capture-policy` | `gateway` read, write |

Request and response shapes are in the [OpenAPI document](/api/openapi/).
Invoke-only permission cannot read or list any of these resources.

## Lifecycle rules

- A repeated `PUT` replaces the named resource; concurrent writers resolve by
  commit order. There are no preconditions or resource versions.
- A credential `PUT` on an active credential rotates it. Revocation is
  terminal: a revoked name cannot be replaced.
- Deleting a credential that a deployment references fails with a conflict.
  Deleting an absent credential or deployment succeeds.
- `DELETE` on the fallback or governance policy restores the default. The
  capture policy defaults to disabled and has no delete.
- Configuration changes apply to calls admitted afterwards; an admitted call
  keeps the configuration snapshot it started with.

## Operation support

A deployment declares the operations it serves in `capabilities`. A
declaration its adapter cannot serve is rejected naming `capabilities`, and a
call no authorized deployment can serve fails before any provider work.
`streaming` operations also accept `stream: true`; `buffered` operations answer
in one response. Batches include the input, output, and error files a batch
requires.

| Adapter | Chat Completions | Responses | Embeddings | Images | Audio | Batches |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| `openai` | streaming | streaming | buffered | buffered | buffered | buffered |
| `openai_compatible` | streaming | streaming | buffered | buffered | buffered | buffered |
| `anthropic` | streaming | — | — | — | — | — |
| `gemini` | streaming | — | — | — | — | — |
| `vertex` | streaming | — | — | — | — | — |

## Credential sources

| Source | Request | Read view |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `environment` | `{"environment": {"binding": "<name>"}}` | the binding name |
| `external_secret` | `{"external_secret": {"backend": "<name>", "reference": "<opaque>"}}` | backend and reference |
| `managed_secret` | `{"managed_secret": {"secret": "<value>"}}` | `"managed_secret"` — the value is never readable |

Bindings and backends are declared by the operator in the
[`[gateway]` configuration](/self-hosting/configuration/#gateway); a tenant
names them and never supplies a variable name, path, or backend address. With
those two sources Wyrd never accepts or stores a credential value.

A `managed_secret` is the one source a tenant supplies directly: the value is
sealed with the tenant's own keyring before it is stored, and no read, list,
log, or error ever returns it. Submit it from the CLI or the scoped MCP tool —
the SDKs project the redacted view but expose no submission helper — and
rotate it by submitting the new value under the same name. The operator must
have configured a keyring for the tenant in
[`gateway.managed_secret_keys`](/self-hosting/configuration/#gateway); without
one, submission is refused and existing envelopes stop resolving rather than
falling back to another key or source.

## From the CLI

```bash
cat > credential.json <<'EOF'
{"name": "openai-primary", "provider": "openai",
 "source": {"environment": {"binding": "openai-prod"}}}
EOF
wyrd gateway credential put --file credential.json
wyrd gateway credential list
```

Submit a managed secret on stdin so the value never reaches a process argument
or a file on disk:

```bash
printf '{"name": "openai-primary", "provider": "openai",
 "source": {"managed_secret": {"secret": "%s"}}}' "$OPENAI_API_KEY" \
  | wyrd gateway credential put --file -
```

The Rust, Python, and TypeScript SDKs keep the redacted credential reads and
the rest of gateway administration, but none of them exposes credential
mutation: no submission, rotation, revocation, or deletion, for any source. The
CLI and the scoped MCP tool are the supported credential lifecycle surfaces in
every language. The [MCP endpoint](/for-agents/) advertises one tool per
operation with the same permissions:

| Kind | Tools | Arguments |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Read-only | `gateway.list_provider_credentials`, `gateway.list_provider_deployments`, `gateway.get_fallback_policy`, `gateway.get_governance_policy`, `gateway.get_capture_policy` | none |
| Read-only | `gateway.get_provider_credential`, `gateway.get_provider_deployment` | `{"name"}` |
| Idempotent replacement | `gateway.put_provider_credential`, `gateway.put_provider_deployment`, `gateway.put_fallback_policy`, `gateway.put_governance_policy`, `gateway.put_capture_policy` | the `PUT` request body |
| Destructive | `gateway.revoke_provider_credential`, `gateway.delete_provider_credential`, `gateway.delete_provider_deployment` | `{"name"}` |
| Destructive | `gateway.delete_fallback_policy`, `gateway.delete_governance_policy` | none |

Listings return `{"items": [...]}` and deletions return `{}`.
